C.push(p([t(`Surprise 1 — weekday ranks ${ord(RK.weekday_LMD)} in LMD and joint last (zero importance) in Mordor (leakage). `,{bold:true}),t(`It is the only LMD feature with a material permutation drop (${ST.lmd_perm_top[1].toFixed(4)}; every other feature < 0.0002) and it has zero importance in Mordor, because it identifies the three LMD attack days (Fig. 2, Table 8); excluded with the other time features.`)]));
C.push(p([t('Surprise 2 — the previous “relational PID” feature is a null-fill artefact. ',{bold:true}),t(`naive_relational_pid = [ProcessId = ExecutionProcessID] compares the EventData subject PID with the header PID of the logging process. The LMD export writes “0” into empty fields; on EID 10 (ProcessAccess) rows both are empty, so equality holds for 100.0% of EID 10 rows (97.7% of all rows on which the feature fires), for at most 0.22% of any other event type with more than 500 rows, and otherwise on 654 rare housekeeping rows (EID 4/16/8/6/255). Its attack mean (${ST.lmd_pid_attack_mean.toFixed(3)}) equals the EID 10 share of attack rows (${ST.lmd_attack_eid10_pct.toFixed(1)}%): an event-type indicator created by the export convention, not lineage; it ranks ${ord(RK.pid_LMD)} on LMD and has zero importance on Mordor, where empty fields are genuinely null.`)]));
C.push(p([t('Surprise 3 — near-perfect fan-out features (modality confound; capture-specific magnitude). ',{bold:true}),t(`Benign LMD is ${ST.lmd_benign_eid3_pct}% network events (EID 3); attack rows are ${ST.lmd_attack_eid_pct['7']}% image loads (EID 7), ${ST.lmd_attack_eid_pct['10']}% process access (EID 10), ${ST.lmd_attack_eid_pct['23']}% file deletes (EID 23) and ${ST.lmd_attack_eid_pct['3']}% network (Fig. 5). A host-event row carries its host’s trailing outbound fan-out, which is small in the attack period (host rows average ${f2(CF.f_distinct_dstip_300s.host_attack_mean)} distinct destinations in attack windows vs ${f2(CF.f_distinct_dstip_300s.host_benign_mean)} in benign ones), so part of the feature’s power is event-type composition. Within network rows only, distinct_dstip_300s still separates (attack ${f2(CF.f_distinct_dstip_300s.net_attack_mean)} vs benign ${f2(CF.f_distinct_dstip_300s.net_benign_mean)} distinct IPs; attack-positive AUC ${f3(CF.f_distinct_dstip_300s.net_auc_attack_pos)}), but far more weakly in Mordor (MI ${ST.mi_dstip.Mordor.toFixed(2)} vs ${ST.mi_dstip.LMD.toFixed(2)}): the LMD magnitude reflects its background-traffic density.`)]));
C.push(p([t('Surprise 4 — host/credential features rank low on LMD (modality sparsity). ',{bold:true}),t(`Features that need ParentImage/CommandLine (EID 1), GrantedAccess (EID 10) or TargetFilename (EID 11) are defined on under ${Math.ceil(ST.lmd_eid1_10_11_pct)}% of LMD rows, so on an unpartitioned matrix they win little global impurity reduction whatever their within-modality value (only image_is_lolbin and exec_from_staging, which read Image on any event, reach more rows). The benign zeros of the credential features (lsass_access ${ST.lmd_lsass_attack_pct.toFixed(2)}% of attack rows) are composition, not specificity: benign LMD holds ${ST.lmd_benign_eid10_rows===1?'a single EID 10 row':ST.lmd_benign_eid10_rows+' EID 10 rows'}, so cred_access_mask’s V = ${ST.lmd_credmask_V.toFixed(2)} (Fig. 10) is the Surprise-3 confound again. The remedy is modality-aware evaluation and per-host aggregates defined on every row (§5), not discarding them.`)]));
C.push(p([t('Multicollinearity and saturation. ',{bold:true}),t('Permutation drops are ≈ 0 for almost every LMD feature because redundant siblings (§3.2) absorb a permuted column and a saturated AUC is insensitive to small changes — hence three importance measures and one composite basis.')]));

