C.push(h2('5.3 Data Scaling and Scaling Remedies'));
C.push(p([t('Continuous rate, fan-out, out-degree and Δt features are right-skewed with burst outliers, so RobustScaler x′ = (x − median)/IQR is used (IQR = 0 → divisor 1): Z-scores are dominated by the outliers and min-max compresses the bulk. Binary flags stay in {0, 1}; event_category is one-hot for distance-based models; the Chapter-4 trees used its integer code, whose arbitrary order can change which splits are found and hence the MDI/gain credited to the column (a caveat on its Table 7 ranks; its class association rests on the order-free Cramér’s V of Table 5 and Figs. 10–11). Protocol: split → fit the scaler on training rows → transform every other set with those medians and IQRs; Milestone-3 imbalance handling (class weights, or any resampler) obeys the same rule. '),t(`Audit (scaling_audit.py, host × day split, ${RT.scaling.continuous_features.length} continuous features): host_evt_rate_60s has training median/IQR ${RT.scaling.LMD.features.f_host_evt_rate_60s.train_median.toLocaleString()}/${RT.scaling.LMD.features.f_host_evt_rate_60s.train_iqr.toLocaleString()} in LMD and ${RT.scaling.Mordor.features.f_host_evt_rate_60s.train_median.toLocaleString()}/${RT.scaling.Mordor.features.f_host_evt_rate_60s.train_iqr.toLocaleString()} in Mordor; and the LMD-fitted transform puts Mordor’s median at ${RT.scaling.cross_fit.f_host_evt_rate_60s.mordor_median_under_LMD_fit.toLocaleString(undefined,{maximumFractionDigits:0})} scaled units — a source-fitted scaler alone does not remove the scale shift; `),t('quantile alignment (Table 10) is the additional step.')]));

